CLAIM AUDIT:

Collection edited by Fullbrook and Morgan â€” SUPPORTED by source [3].

Title of the collection ("23 Responses to Economics' Biggest-ever Question") and subtitle ("Economics and the Biophysical Limits to Economic Growth") â€” SUPPORTED by source [3].

"For over a century economics has loosely guided the global economy's national economies" â€” SUPPORTED; direct quotation from source [3].

"the global economy caused the climate crisis, and the crisis, now in an accelerating phase, threatens the economy's own existence" â€” SUPPORTED; source [3] states "Natural science tells us that the climate crisis has been both caused by the global economy and ultimately threatens its existence. Because that crisis is now in an accelerating phase."

Green growth as the assumption embedded in the European Green Deal, Paris Agreement, IPCC reports, and the US Inflation Reduction Act â€” SUPPORTED by source [1].

Green growth "promoted by leading multilateral organizations" â€” SUPPORTED by source [1].

"treated as common sense" â€” source [1] says "for many, it has become common sense"; the draft's rendering is faithful.

Winne van Woerden as author of the Uneven Earth piece â€” SUPPORTED by source [1].

AI risk as "the apex of growth mania" â€” SUPPORTED; direct quotation from source [2].

Tech elites making decisions "without the consent or interests of the broader population" â€” SUPPORTED; source [2] states "They take these risks without our consent and without our interests in mind."

Potential replacement of humanity as Earth's most intelligent species â€” SUPPORTED; source [2].

"The Fullbrook and Morgan collection does not address AI" â€” UNSUPPORTED and invented. Source [3]'s summary is a truncated blog post introduction. The available evidence establishes only what the collection's framing premise is; it does not describe the collection's contents or exclusions. The writer cannot know from the supplied evidence what the 23 responses do or do not address. This claim is a negative-scope assertion with no basis in the packet.

---

REQUIRED REPAIRS:

Remove the "The Fullbrook and Morgan collection does not address AI" sentence. It is invented.

Remove or substantially reframe the AI/growth mania section. If source [2] is to be used at all, it must connect to the limits-to-growth thesis the piece is organized around, not stand as an isolated note from a different beat.

Expand the body to â‰¥ 600 words by developing material already present in the packet. Source [1] contains a substantive argument about decoupling â€” the ecomodernist premise that there are no limits to growth because there are no limits to human ingenuity, and the critique of that premise â€” which could support at least one additional section. The implications of the Fullbrook/Morgan collection for how mainstream models treat resource constraints and ecological feedback is the analytical core the piece gestures at but never builds.

---

PIPELINE EVIDENCE:

No prior production run exists (PRODUCTION RUN: null). This is a cold first draft. The deterministic-standards reading reports a single gate failure: body-too-short (362 words, needs â‰¥ 600). No SEO or citation gate failures are recorded; those likely passed the mechanical checks described in article.mts, or the body-length failure was a blocking condition that halted further gate reporting.

The research packet selected 4 sources from 12 candidates. Source [4] (renewable energy persistence, score 8) had essentially no thematic relevance to "limits to growth" and contributed nothing to the draft. Source [2] (AI and growth mania, score 51) contributed only the tangential AI section. The load-bearing sources are [1] and [3]. Source [3]'s summary is truncated at a "[â€¦]" â€” the writer had access to a blog post introduction but not the collection's actual contents. This is the realistic ceiling on what can be known from the packet about the Fullbrook/Morgan volume itself.

The writer model's response to thin primary material was to reach for source [2] on keyword overlap ("growth") rather than develop the analytical frame available in source [1]. That choice produced the non-sequitur section and the invented scope claim.

---

PIPELINE IMPROVEMENT:

The assignment layer correctly identified the Fullbrook/Morgan announcement as the primary source for this term/beat. The gap is that source [3]'s summary is a truncated blog post teaser, not an extractable content summary. A packet built on an announcement-only source summary will predictably underperform on word count regardless of writer capability. A scoring or pre-selection step that distinguishes between article summaries (extractable argument) and announcement summaries (metadata only) could flag this before the writer is invoked and trigger a "reporting needed" obstruction rather than an underdeveloped draft.

If the packet proceeds to the writer as is, the writer instructions should more explicitly prohibit pivoting to tangential context sources when the primary source's summary is short: the correct response to thin primary material is to develop the available analytical frame more fully from the secondary source (here, source [1]), not to import an off-thesis source and then note its irrelevance in the body.

The invented scope claim ("the collection does not address AI") is a specific error class â€” asserting what a source does not contain, from a truncated summary. This is harder to catch deterministically, but the claim-audit gate should surface it: the claim's source is the writer's inference, not any supplied evidence, and it fails the "do not add categories or scope that the admitted sources do not state" law already in the voice configuration.

---

END REPORT:

